Part 1 (1 mark): Agree. Under Ind AS 1, only material accounting policy information must be disclosed; policy information about immaterial transactions need not be. If OTL's repairs and upgrades are immaterial in both amount and nature, leaving out the capitalisation policy is not a non-compliance. (If upgrades are large, such as technology upgrades, the policy becomes material.)

Part 2 (4 marks): Accounting policy information is material if, read with the other information in the financial statements, it could reasonably be expected to influence the decisions of primary users of general purpose financial statements. It is likely to be material when it relates to material transactions, events or conditions and:
(a) the entity changed its accounting policy during the period and the change materially changed the financial statements;
(b) the entity chose the policy from options permitted by Ind AS (e.g., the cost or revaluation model);
(c) the policy was developed under Ind AS 8 because no Ind AS specifically applies;
(d) the policy relates to an area needing significant judgements or assumptions, disclosed under paras 122 and 125;
(e) the accounting is complex and users would not otherwise understand the transactions (e.g., more than one Ind AS applies to a class of transactions).
Also, entity-specific information is more useful than standardised text that repeats Ind AS.
